{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQV1nkEjCcf5ygUcYwr8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Got it: percentage, applied before VAT. Here's the full spec with those two answers in. Three points are still open and marked ❓.\n\n**Field**\n- `order.discountPercent`: a number from 0 to 100. Missing means 0.\n- ❓ Decimals like `12.5` allowed? My recommendation is yes.\n- Anything outside 0–100, or not a number, throws an error.\n\n**Calculation** (all in cents)\n- `discountCents = round(subtotal × pct / 100)`\n- `taxCents = round((subtotal − discountCents) × 0.2)`\n- `totalCents = subtotal − discountCents + taxCents`\n\n**Invoice**\n- Adds a `discountPercent` and a `discountCents` line, so subtotal − discount + tax = total still holds.\n\n**Refunds**\n- Each line gets the same percentage off: `discounted = net − round(net × pct / 100)`, and the refund is `discounted + round(discounted × 0.2)`.\n- ❓ Rounding is done per line, so refunding every line separately can come out a cent different from what the order charged in total. My recommendation is to accept that for now and write it down in a comment. The alternative is to keep a running total of what's been refunded and adjust the last refund so the totals match exactly. That's more work, and it means refunds would have to remember earlier refunds.\n\n**Tests**\n- Discounted invoice reconciles.\n- VAT is charged on the discounted amount.\n- Refund on a discounted order.\n- Invalid percentages are rejected.\n- Existing tests still pass, since no discount means 0%.\n\n❓ **Where the discount lives:** I'd put the discount logic in `order.js`, in a new `discount(order)` function, so tax, invoice and refund all use the same calculation.\n\nIf you're happy with my recommendations on the three ❓ points, say go and I'll build it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_token